// A read of a path that is not there fails with a structured IOError (SZ4005)
// and a non-zero exit, including when the path contains `..`.
//
// This does NOT prove a traversal guard, and it never did. There is no such
// guard: `File` is accepted but inert (security.md), so `File.read("../x")`
// reaches outside the working directory and succeeds. This program used to say
// "path traversal must be rejected" and "an attacker might try to escape the
// sandbox", and it passed only because the path it named does not exist — on
// Windows, on Linux and on macOS alike. A security test that passes for an
// unrelated reason is worse than no test, because the suite then reads as proof.
//
// The real behaviour is recorded in tests/filesystem_reach.rs, so confining
// `File` later has to be a deliberate change with a test to update.
let content = File.read("../__no_such_file_for_the_path_tests__");
out "FAIL: reading a path that is not there should have failed";
